SM(300,{"n":"Exodus 20.18-26","a":"Fr Stephen De Young Archives/Exodus 20.18-26.mp3","d":2845.3,"s":[[0.24,"Come and study the Holy Scriptures with us as Father Stephen DeYoung teaches verse by verse on the podcast The Whole Counsel of God."],[8.96,"Father Stephen holds a PhD in Biblical Studies, and is an Orthodox priest serving at Archangel Gabriel Orthodox Church in Lafayette, Louisiana."],[20.96,"We'll go ahead and get started."],[23.76,"And when we get started, which shouldn't be too long from me saying this."],[29.36,"We're going to be picking up in Exodus chapter 20, verse 18."],[38.32,"But I say it's not going to be that long from now because this is going to be a quick recap, because last time we talked about the Ten Commandments."],[46.08,"Now there's only sort of nine of them, but we went through them, but I'm not going through them again, because that wouldn't be a recap."],[51.6,"That would just be repeating ourselves."],[53.84,"So, but that's what we went talked about last time."],[57.44,"And um how the Ten Commandments are sort of a microcosm or or a summary in a certain sense of what now the whole covenant with Israel is going to be."],[73.0,"So, what we're gonna get now in part, there's gonna be a whole bunch of things through the rest of the Torah."],[78.76,"So we have a whole bunch of different kinds of things."],[80.68,"There's still gonna be narrative parts like the narratives we've been reading reading, where things are gonna happen as Israel continues through the desert."],[88.2,"So there's still gonna be stories like that."],[91.24,"There's also gonna be now commandments that are gonna be sort of breaking down in more detail the things that we saw summarized sort of in the Ten Commandments."],[101.8,"Uh, there's also gonna be liturgical instructions."],[106.16,"A big part of the rest of the book of Exodus, which everyone is going to thoroughly enjoy, I know, is going to be the detailed instructions for building all the furniture for the tabernacle."],[116.72,"How many cubits wide and how many cubits high."],[135.32,"As we go through the rest of the Torah."],[137.56,"For tonight, I think we're pretty much going to be in the commandments section, stemming from the Ten Commandments, sort of breaking down some of these things in more detail."],[155.32,"So, yeah. So hopefully, the first time I went through this many years ago, like 11 and a half years ago, maybe 12, I kind of had in my head that, well, as soon as I get into the end of Exodus, and especially when I get into Leviticus, everybody's gonna leave."],[175.72,"But everyone did not leave that time, and I hope they you guys don't all leave this time."],[181.56,"Because even though, yes, some of this stuff, you know, when you're waving the fatty part above the kidney, you know, of the calf in the air and stuff, may seem less exciting than a lot of the things. we've talked about so far"],[196.88,"Uh there is some actual exciting stuff mixed in there, or at least more exciting, and there is something that's being said with that kind of stuff too."],[207.44,"Uh, but when we're in the sort of repetitive bits, or tedious or super detailed bits, like listing all the birds that are unclean, we're gonna move through that relatively quickly."],[219.36,"So hopefully it will still be a good experience for everyone."],[223.76,"Um, all that said, unless anybody has any leftover uh questions or comments or stories of political candidacies gone awry, we'll get started."],[237.28,"I heard that a qubit is the length from your middle finger to your elbow."],[239.92,"Yes, that's true"],[242.2,"Yes. How would they standardize then for like they didn't, that's one of the problems."],[249.0,"Yeah, your qubit might be longer than my human."],[253.8,"So we'll talk about that more when we get there, but yeah."],[257.0,"Yeah, it wasn't really standardized."],[260.84,"You could build something big."],[262.92,"Yeah. Uh later on, it did get quasi-standardized, much later than this."],[268.76,"And like most things, sort of like with we use, you know, if you're using English measurements, feet."],[275.58,"It's like, well, that's the length of your foot."],[277.42,"Well, people have different size feet."],[279.18,"It was the king's foot."],[281.72,"So that sort of happens later on in the history."],[286.32,"You get the the uh royal and then the imperial qubit."],[292.97,"Based on uh the the quote unquote perfect man who is heading your so one quintessor It was perfectly proportioned no matter what the measurement you can use."],[306.09,"Is it built to spec as far as the qubit?"],[309.61,"I have no idea what size qubit he used."],[313.37,"I'm dubious of the whole thing since it got water damaged in the rain."],[319.53,"That's true. It was water damaged in a storm."],[322.49,"Really? Yeah. Oh recently?"],[325.21,"I don't know."],[325.81,"A year and a half ago."],[326.93,"Oh, good. So I think they must have messed up somewhere."],[328.25,"Um Just saying."],[335.41,"So we'll go ahead and uh pick up at Exodus chapter 20, verse 18."],[342.53,"Now all the people witnessed the thunderings, the lightning flashes, the sound of the trumpet, and the mountain smoking."],[348.77,"And when the people saw this, they trembled and stood afar off."],[354.37,"So remember, they had been warned, you know, don't go near the mountain."],[359.41,"You go near the mountain, you're gonna die."],[363.09,"They they don't didn't need the warnings, turns out."],[365.89,"They're like, we're not going anywhere near that mountain because we're gonna die."],[369.14,"Um, but so they see Thunder Lightning, all these things."],[372.66,"Hear the blowing of trumpets coming from the top of the mountain"],[377.64,"They're scared and want nothing, nothing to do with it."],[381.1,"And we talked about how this is the flip side of we talk about how coming into the presence of God is a sinful person is dangerous."],[390.08,"This is the flip side of that, which is when we see people experiencing God's presence in the Bible."],[397.13,"It's not like they're, oh, I want to run up and give God a hug."],[401.81,"Uh it's, you know, they're terrified."],[406.63,"They rip their clothes, they're, you know, um, they even start doing that when an angel shows up, like, let alone God Himself."],[413.83,"So and that's the reason for that."],[418.11,"The reason for that is not that you know, God is mean, goes around smiting people, it's that God's presence brings about an awareness of the people's own sinfulness."],[431.12,"And sort of humbles them very quickly, so that they don't want to come near."],[441.37,"Then they said to Moses, you speak with us, and we will hear, but let not God speak with us lest we die."],[449.39,"So there's the right."],[451.95,"Um not only do we not want to come directly to his presence, not only do we not want to go try and go up there and see him."],[459.03,"If he even speaks to us directly, we might die."],[463.85,"So this is this is the idea that's going to that we saw get referred to in Hebrews."],[470.27,"Of Moses being the mediator of this covenant."],[473.23,"This is what it means that he's the mediator."],[476.48,"And this is important because people will also say Christ is the mediator of the new covenant."],[481.92,"But a lot of times when they say that, they mean something totally different."],[486.17,"Because they're talking about like Christ dying for our sins in the new covenant."],[489.53,"Well, Moses didn't die for anyone's sins."],[492.35,"Moses is an he's the mediator."],[495.17,"God talks to him, he talks to the people."],[499.41,"So that's the sense in which mediator is being used here"],[504.57,"It's not that people worshiped Moses, and now we worship Jesus."],[508.89,"Because he's the that's not how any of this works."],[511.57,"Um it's because of their awareness of their own."],[518.82,"And so um this is this is why when Christ starts being spoken of as mediator in the New Testament, there's a shift."],[533.0,"This is why it means why people talk about Christ as mediator differently, is that Christ is not sort of the buffer. between humans and God"],[546.8,"Contra some reformation theories about the relationship between Christ and God and people."],[554.8,"It's not that God is going to wipe people out with his presence, but Christ comes in between and God talks to Jesus and Jesus talks to us with Moses."],[568.88,"Christ isn't the buffer."],[570.48,"Christ does the almost the opposite."],[572.9,"He reconciles people to God."],[574.26,"He brings people to God and presents them to God so that they can safely come into the presence of God themselves."],[583.82,"So that's a fundamental difference between Moses' role in the old covenant and Christ's role in the new covenant."],[591.98,"So he's not the mediator the way that I was brought up here, and Jesus is the mediator."],[597.5,"Maybe not, depending on how they were teaching that."],[612.78,"So that humans don't get it."],[615.02,"My my prayers pass through him to God."],[617.26,"Well, no, he is God."],[618.62,"Okay, so then that was all wrong."],[620.54,"That's the same thing about that teaching, too"],[624.46,"So um, yeah, no, we pray what Jesus teaches in the Gospels is that you pray to God in his name."],[631.5,"Yeah, okay But that's not like I can't pray to God the Father."],[638.77,"Christ says that very explicitly in St."],[640.29,"John's Gospel. The Father Himself loves you."],[646.13,"So I don't have to start my prayer with Jesus."],[648.69,"I can say God."],[651.17,"Yeah. I got you."],[652.93,"Yeah. Um the the prayers in the liturgy, by and large, there are some that are directed to Jesus, but most of them are directed to God the Father."],[663.73,"Yeah. And then in the name of the Trinity."],[666.41,"And you could tell who they're directed to, because at the end of the prayer, it'll say, Thou and thine only begotten Son and thine only spirit."],[672.99,"Well, the thou there is. God the Father then"],[677.11,"Well, it seems like when I pray out of the prayer book, I'm doing like that, but then I've noticed when I'm just praying uh by myself, without it, it's always been like, you know, Lord Jesus."],[689.11,"Yeah. You know, so it's all it's kind of ingrained in me."],[692.39,"So that's right."],[694.2,"So Moses is a mediator in the sense that he's an accommodation to a problem."],[702.52,"Remember, we have holy God, we have sinful people."],[705.97,"And so Moses' mediator here is sort of accommodating to that."],[710.45,"He is right to that problem."],[712.45,"So he is a buffer, then."],[714.13,"Yeah, Moses is."],[716.58,"But Christ. solves that problem by making the sinful people no longer a sinful people"],[723.34,"So they can come into the presence of God."],[728.46,"So he solves the underlying problem."],[730.62,"You could still call him the mediator of the covenant, but you mean something different by that."],[735.26,"Okay. He's not running interference."],[738.66,"Or serving as a buffer."],[740.76,"Or absorbing God's wrath or any of those things."],[744.16,"He's reconciling us to God so that we can now come into God's presence."],[758.96,"Verse 20, Moses said to to the people, be of good courage, for God has come test you, that his fear may be among you, so you may not sin."],[768.68,"So the people stood afar off, but Moses drew near the thick darkness where God was."],[789.09,"Right now you have what we refer to as the fear of God."],[792.79,"This awe, this reverence, this awareness of your own sinfulness and humility."],[799.68,"When you're tempted to sin."],[802.21,"Remember how you're feeling right now."],[803.65,"Remember who God is."],[806.45,"That may help you not do that."],[812.46,"Um, St. John Chrysostom at one point got it this another way."],[817.92,"He said, uh we go out and we sin sort of without fear."],[825.7,"And without shame."],[827.62,"And then when we have to go to confession, we're afraid and we're ashamed, about going to confession."],[834.79,"And he says, that should be the exact opposite."],[839.13,"We should be afraid and ashamed to sin."],[845.14,"And we should be happy."],[847.72,"And be bold when we go to confession."],[851.66,"Where we're going to get forgiven."],[853.82,"But we have that sort of backwards."],[856.38,"And that's similar to what Moses is saying here."],[861.58,"Similar to what."],[862.7,"And this is important in terms of conditioning what we mean by fear of the Lord."],[865.9,"That doesn't mean being afraid of God."],[868.14,"That doesn't mean sitting around thinking God's going to smite you, God's going to kill you."],[872.86,"Moses is not endorsing, like, yes, heathens, stay away."],[877.5,"From the right"],[881.08,"But the fear of the Lord is that awe and reverence of him for who he is."],[888.94,"The the and uh it prevents, so in the context of the ancient world, you look at the way pagans dealt with idols, they were very comfortable going and doing rituals."],[906.87,"Because they do, hey, we go do this ritual, we give food to the idol, we dress it, we do this stuff, and then we get goodies in return."],[915.53,"So, there's no reason to be scared, walking into an idol's temple."],[922.93,"In fact, they were so familiar that sometimes they would like to blame them."],[927.99,"So when Augustus lost a legion in the forest of Germany, for months the Romans would go and throw rocks at the temples of the gods."],[938.19,"Because they're like, hey, we've been offering you all these sacrifices and stuff, and you guys didn't deliver when it counted."],[944.68,"That's how comfortable they were."],[948.92,"This still goes on in Latin America where they'll take uh an image of the saint that hasn't answered their prayers and put it in in a prison or something like that."],[960.52,"Yeah. Um maybe some idolatry going on."],[965.56,"Anyway, um, but you know, for us, more often, for us here, we're not gonna do that."],[974.75,"But we are very capable of developing an overfamiliarity, sort of presumptuous attitude toward God, toward his church, toward holy things."],[986.29,"Where we just sort of treat them casually."],[990.1,"And, you know, and when it comes to the particulars of what Moses is talking about here with sinning, we can become very casual about that too."],[999.06,"Oh, well, God'll forgive me."],[1001.22,"I'm only human."],[1004.26,"You know, it's not a big deal, whatever."],[1008.03,"Everybody's, you know, everybody does this at some point."],[1013.44,"That's the kind of over casual."],[1015.6,"That Proverbs tells us"],[1015.88,"That's the opposite of the fear of the Lord."],[1020.84,"We'll get to Proverbs eventually."],[1022.38,"Is the beginning of wisdom."],[1024.38,"Meaning, if you don't have that, you're a fool."],[1028.7,"If we don't start there, if we don't start with this odd reference toward God, you're a fool, you can't claim to be wise about or know anything about anything."],[1036.88,"That's the prerequisite."],[1038.88,"Once you have that, that's the beginning."],[1041.2,"Now we can start talking about wisdom in terms of how to live and how to behave."],[1049.12,"It's the opposite"],[1050.14,"The fear of the Lord is the beginning of wisdom, is the opposite of the fool says in his heart that there is no God."],[1055.27,"There weren't any atheists when Proverbs was written."],[1058.63,"They didn't exist."],[1059.11,"I'm an atheist"],[1060.93,"So there is no God there is not saying I don't believe in God."],[1065.81,"It's saying you don't have that reverence."],[1069.97,"You don't have that awe, you don't have that fear of God."],[1073.01,"You act as if there wasn't a God in the world."],[1078.61,"So it's kind of the opposite of a modern atheist."],[1081.01,"Modern atheists going around, go around claiming they don't believe in God, but then they kind of, if you look at their morality and stuff, they act like there actually is one."],[1090.77,"They give money to charity and they do all these things, even though they claim not to believe in God."],[1095.11,"This is the opposite."],[1096.31,"This is right."],[1097.75,"People who would say if you ask them, oh yeah, I of course there's a God."],[1103.97,"And I make the sacrifices or I do whatever, but then they live as if there isn't."],[1111.57,"There's not gonna be there's not gonna be any justice, there's not gonna be any judgment at the end of our life, there's not gonna be right."],[1117.65,"We could just drink and be merry."],[1120.55,"Like that's do what we want."],[1121.99,"But we're also not you said we're not supposed to uh like nonchalantly like oh you know, I'm human, but yet you're not supposed to beat yourself up."],[1132.55,"No, you're supposed to repent."],[1134.31,"Yeah, and and feeling bad and being emo, like I'm cutting your chest."],[1140.47,"No, there's not a fine line, it's two different things."],[1142.63,"Oh, okay. It's two different things, yeah."],[1146.6,"Repenting and feeling bad are two different things."],[1149.36,"Feeling bad might motivate some people to repent."],[1154.08,"Might. Some people."],[1156.22,"There's a lot of people who just sort of wallow in feeling bad."],[1162.14,"Despondency and despair is a vice."],[1165.18,"It's a sin."],[1166.22,"It's a sin itself."],[1168.86,"Yeah. So that's not repentance, obviously."],[1172.42,"That's more sin-in."],[1173.96,"So repentance, what is repentance?"],[1175.88,"Repentance is you have to go and actually do things."],[1179.41,"You have to try to make up for."],[1180.93,"We're gonna see as we get into some of these commandments."],[1183.57,"There's gonna be restitution."],[1185.73,"You have to go and make it right as best you can."],[1189.59,"Try and fix what you broke."],[1191.83,"Try and help heal the people you hurt."],[1193.67,"Not just say sorry to the people you hurt, but try to repair as best you can that relationship."],[1203.77,"As best you can."],[1205.21,"And it takes time."],[1206.59,"You could you could hurt someone in a second."],[1209.01,"And it'll take a lot more in a second to heal that again."],[1213.3,"That takes time to happen."],[1216.34,"You have to do that."],[1217.38,"And then you have to try to stop doing in the future, to the best of your ability, whatever it was you just did."],[1224.7,"In addition to trying to fix it, you have to stop doing it."],[1228.4,"And making that effort to do both of those things. that's what repentance is not feeling bad"],[1235.48,"You could do both of those things and never feel bad."],[1240.12,"You could be joyful and do both of those things."],[1243.48,"The joy comes from the fact that you know you're you're gonna be forgiven."],[1248.84,"I got you."],[1249.72,"So it's right to uh kind of force yourself away from that feeling bad and just keep moving forward."],[1255.32,"Yes, yes, yeah, and one of the biggest distractions to actual repentance and healing is wanting people to feel bad."],[1265.45,"Because we put that onto other people."],[1269.21,"Someone has hurt us, someone has has done something legitimately sinned against us."],[1274.01,"Mother's really good at that, and we in response want them to feel bad."],[1280.42,"That's a kind of revenge."],[1283.54,"Yeah. We want them to feel bad, we want them to be miserable."],[1287.76,"Because we feel bad."],[1288.96,"They made us feel bad."],[1290.16,"They made us miserable."],[1291.28,"They hurt us."],[1292.48,"And so we want them to feel bad."],[1298.4,"Yeah, my mom does that."],[1299.97,"Well, it's not, and it's not just on an individual level."],[1303.49,"When you're dealing with a community."],[1306.03,"People will spend hours and hours arguing about whose fault a problem is."],[1310.75,"Yeah. Rather than fixing it."],[1315.89,"Rather than saying, who cares whose fault it is?"],[1319.54,"Or someone just stepping up and raising their hand and saying, okay, it's my fault."],[1323.3,"Now can we fix it?"],[1325.46,"Like, everybody blame me."],[1327.06,"Okay, now can we fix it."],[1331.43,"Like that's and frankly, all that comes from a really bad Western view of sin and guilt."],[1341.27,"And I've said before, and I'll say many times, I'm sure, before I die, provided I live very long, guilt is not helpful."],[1350.39,"In any sense of the word."],[1353.19,"The feeling of guilt, assigning guilt to someone, not helpful."],[1359.67,"It runs counter to repentance, usually."],[1361.59,"If you understand that repentance is healing and fixing things, repairing things, reconciling people and moving on, then pointing fingers at yourself or other people doesn't help with that."],[1376.45,"It just doesn't."],[1377.97,"Sometimes feeling guilty can be a way to be more self absorbed."],[1383.23,"You wallow in it."],[1385.23,"And it leads to despondency and despair and all kinds just the pride to feel, you know, despondent and to flip."],[1398.59,"Yeah. Like all bad things, God can use it for good."],[1403.01,"So God can use your guilt to bring you to repentance in some cases."],[1405.65,"But the devil can also use guilt as a lever"],[1411.32,"Because even after you come to confession and confess something, he can show up the next day and say, hey, remember when you did that?"],[1421.34,"You're no good."],[1422.94,"Keep tormenting with you with it the rest of your life."],[1426.46,"Even though you repented and God's forgiven and God's wanting to heal you."],[1432.12,"He gets you to not take advantage of that healing because you just sit and wallow in that guilt that he's spoken you with over and over again."],[1441.0,"Instead of getting about repenting and being healed."],[1444.84,"So guilt in that sense is not. useful"],[1452.38,"And we're gonna see there's nothing about feelings of guilt anywhere in any of these commandments."],[1459.19,"It's if someone does this, here's what they need to do to fix it."],[1463.33,"Here's what they need to do to make restitution."],[1467.57,"Nothing about feeling bad."],[1470.05,"Nothing even about apologizing."],[1473.65,"Which we love, apologies."],[1475.51,"People go on apology tours in the media, you know."],[1478.79,"So, where do you think this idea of like you, your emotions having to match your actions came from?"],[1487.43,"Because it seems sort of like an extreme form of what Christ is saying, where he's kind of like all the time in the Gospels, he's trying to get people who are acting right, uh have their intent right, like having having the right intention and the actions line up."],[1502.11,"It seems like he's always trying to do that knowledge."],[1503.87,"Yeah, but there's nothing about feelings."],[1505.73,"But I mean, so where do you think it where does it come from?"],[1508.53,"It's mostly it mostly comes out of uh existential philosophy in the early 20th century."],[1515.73,"There's precursors to it."],[1518.13,"Like Thomas Hobbes, when he when he talks about ethics, he's got two springs of human action, either you're self-centered or other-centered."],[1526.82,"So that brings motives into it."],[1529.46,"But in terms of emotions, we're talking about like existential philosophy in the 20th century."],[1534.98,"So intent comes in earlier than that."],[1535.78,"Like motives comes in earlier than that"],[1541.58,"So Hobbes, for example, he has a very dim view of humanity."],[1545.98,"And so he uses that to say, well, okay, yes, you went and gave to the poor, but if you did that so that you would have treasures in heaven, see that wasn't really a good action."],[1556.86,"It was selfish."],[1560.7,"So you get that level of analysis."],[1564.06,"A lot of that comes out of a Calvinist framework of total depravity."],[1570.44,"And so total depravity."],[1572.46,"What rather, once you hold that, the problem for you is that sometimes people do good things, and so you have to explain how all the things people do are never actually good."],[1578.98,"And so you go after both"],[1583.0,"Okay. I remember that."],[1587.32,"No, Hobbes is uh 17th century."],[1589.24,"Yeah. Okay. And and then but 20th century is where you get into the emotions and feelings things because you get this idea coming out of Heidegger and Heidegger didn't interpret it in terms of emotions, but Sartre kind of did."],[1601.64,"This idea of authenticity."],[1604.36,"Okay. I can see how that would be right that you have to be your authentic self."],[1609.8,"And if you're not emotions aren't matching up with your actions, you're not authentic."],[1614.88,"And so doing the right thing, even though you don't feel like it and don't want to is not seen as doing the right thing anymore."],[1622.44,"Which is really backwards because it's like it takes if you're not feeling it but still doing it, that's more virtuous than if you thought."],[1623.48,"of The basis ethics for most of history."],[1633.46,"Freud played a part in this too, in terms of his influence on the existentials."],[1639.46,"The basis of ethics for most of human history was based around it wasn't always expressed as duty, but the idea that there's a right thing to do, and being a moral person means you do that right thing even if you don't feel like it, even if you're scared, even if you're whatever, you do the right thing anyway."],[1664.63,"And so Freud, Freud's part. in this is that Freud turns things around so that humans have instincts and desires"],[1674.75,"And if those instincts aren't followed and those desires aren't satisfied, that is a problem."],[1684.0,"Before that, the fact that I didn't go out and satisfy all my desires was what made me a moral person."],[1691.16,"I desire to punch a guy in the face because he insulted me."],[1694.92,"I don't. That makes me a moral person."],[1698.05,"I desire to be sexually profligate."],[1701.41,"I'm not. That makes me a moral person."],[1705.63,"And that's what I'm saying."],[1707.07,"But Freud says, oh no. if you have all these sexual desires and you don't fulfill them, that's gonna cause all of these other psychological problems"],[1715.19,"You're gonna be a maladjusted person."],[1717.43,"Da-da-da-da-da-da-da-da-da. Or if you have all this anger because you didn't hit that guy."],[1725.47,"If you have all this anger, uh that's you know gonna cause all these problems, the sublimated anger."],[1732.93,"And so that flows out of the existence of philosophy."],[1735.65,"The goal is to be authentic."],[1739.3,"The goal is to be your authentic self all the time."],[1742.74,"And that is BS."],[1744.94,"Well, yeah, because sometimes my I'm a sinner."],[1749.22,"Yeah. So my authentic self is pretty ugly."],[1751.46,"Yeah And the fix for that is not for me to try to get everyone in society to tell me, no, your authentic self is really beautiful."],[1766.73,"It's for me to be less ugly."],[1770.17,"It's for me to change."],[1772.97,"And work hard and change and try to be less ugly."],[1777.79,"And not show my worst and ugliest bits to everybody all the time."],[1783.53,"The ones I'm still working on."],[1785.69,"I don't have to display those in public."],[1788.34,"L,ike I can keep those private and work on them."],[1792.6,"Um, but yeah, so that's that's this whole idea"],[1798.8,"But yeah, and that's why as you go through the 20th century, like, I would wager to say at this point, 2024."],[1809.59,"Who knows when someone will be listening to this recording?"],[1811.67,"But right now it's 2024."],[1813.51,"This will seem quaint and strange later."],[1816.23,"Um if someone said, you know, I don't have the same feelings for my spouse that I did when I married them five years ago."],[1830.95,"But I'm going to stay in this marriage because it's the right thing to do."],[1834.55,"I think most people in our society here in the United States would say that person is doing the wrong thing."],[1845.71,"Not only would they say you're not obligated to do that anything, they would say they're doing the wrong thing by not leaving and looking for their own happiness somewhere else."],[1858.27,"Yeah. That's why our divorce rate is what it is in the United States today."],[1875.47,"So that is this weird thing that has crept in and has has shifted. things around"],[1882.71,"And now it's got people feeling guilty for doing what is essentially the right thing."],[1890.79,"Well, feeling guilty in spite of doing the right thing."],[1893.03,"Yeah. I've been that down a route where I've been so analytical and been like, well, what's my motivation here?"],[1898.79,"When that's kind of yeah, so that thing there that I've been taught is a lie."],[1904.95,"Yes. Uh if you don't have the right attitude towards it, then it wasn't don't worry about it."],[1909.75,"I got you."],[1910.23,"That's a lot of things."],[1910.87,"Do the right thing."],[1911.75,"I've been taught that do the thing that is right."],[1915.75,"Do the thing that God calls you to do."],[1918.41,"Follow Christ, whether it's hard or easy."],[1921.46,"Whether you want to or not, whether you're having a good time or not."],[1924.82,"or not. Whether it feels natural to you, because it won't"],[1931.48,"Whether you uh you know are doing it authentic or not, and the authenticity will come after doing it."],[1939.0,"Yes, because you'll be transformed."],[1940.52,"That is true."],[1941.4,"Yes. Who you are will be changed by it."],[1944.84,"Okay, so it's and then it will start to become more natural for you."],[1949.16,"Yeah. But that's along a process."],[1955.23,"And Carl Young is uh along with that too."],[1958.05,"Well, he's a whole other kettle of fish."],[1960.29,"We won't get into Fraud, whatn't he?"],[1962.77,"Whole other kettle of Gnostic fish."],[1964.53,"Um they have a big split."],[1967.81,"So verse 22"],[1971.45,"See, I said we'd move quickly through this."],[1977.66,"Then the Lord said to Moses, Thus you shall say to the house of Jacob, and report it, report it to the children of Israel."],[1984.86,"You have seen that I talked with you from heaven."],[1987.66,"You shall not make for yourselves gods of silver and gods of gold."],[1991.66,"You shall not make for yourselves."],[1994.3,"Okay, we'll stop there."],[1995.82,"So this is we're expanding on the beginning of the Ten Commandments."],[2001.08,"Now, uh here's one of our first commandments we're getting now in the in the breakdown."],[2009.18,"The more specific commandments."],[2011.18,"And I'mm pausing here because this shows us here the level of literalis that we should apply to them."],[2020.26,"This is a problem in our culture, the United States mainly."],[2024.9,"But there are a lot of people who are like, no, we have to interpret the Bible literally."],[2027.78,"You have to interpret it literally."],[2029.22,"Okay? Well, if we're gonna be super literal here, then God is saying it's fine to make idols as long as they aren't made of gold or silver."],[2038.24,"Read that sentence again."],[2040.4,"I guess if you make them out of wood or clay, it's cool."],[2043.68,"Or bronze, or copper, or right."],[2047.6,"No, obviously not."],[2050.82,"Obviously not. So this shows us, or should show us right off the bat. that we need to be looking at this"],[2058.7,"And this is, by the way, to go back a few years of the Bible study, whatever reading St."],[2064.22,"Paul, when St."],[2065.02,"Paul talks about the letter and the spirit of the law, this is what he's talking about."],[2070.55,"This is what he's talking about."],[2072.47,"So if someone came to you and said, Yeah, my idol's made of clay, I didn't break the break the commandment, you would be like, okay, maybe not the letter."],[2082.01,"But the Spirit of God who is speaking through this commandment, you definitely violated what he was teaching you by making this idol."],[2091.1,"And so we need to understand this as we go through on all levels."],[2095.94,"Because we're gonna get, it's not gonna be too long, it's gonna be tonight, I think, that we're gonna get to our first."],[2098.62,"I mean, who knows with me, but tonight, hopefully, we're gonna get to our first death penalty"],[2106.18,"Anyone who does this will be put to death."],[2110.36,"And there are people out there who are tempted to say, oh, they executed everyone who did this."],[2117.64,"Okay. Let me assure you, one of them is cursing your mother or father."],[2125.0,"Yikes. Do you think that they actually put to death every child who at any point in their life cursed their mother or father?"],[2138.44,"That would pretty well wipe out the population."],[2140.82,"Hit no. There wouldn't have been many Israelites after a few generations."],[2142.7,"Okay Now they'll point and say, Lo, look, look, the letter."],[2151.38,"It says anyone who does this will surely be put to death."],[2155.94,"We'll talk more when we get there."],[2158.22,"But we already see that's not how we're to read these."],[2163.36,"That really wouldn'tly literal thing is not how we're to read these."],[2171.84,"Right from the first one."],[2173.76,"Does it make sense to say, oh, he just doesn't want them made of gold and silver?"],[2185.37,"Verse 24, an altar of earth you shall make for me, and you shall sacrifice on it your whole burnt offerings, your peace offerings, your sheep and your calves."],[2193.77,"In every place where I record my name, I will come to you and bless you."],[2198.25,"If you make me an altar of stone, you shall not build it of hewn stone, for if you use your tool on it, you defile it."],[2206.01,"Nor shall you go up by steps to my altar that your nakedness may not be exposed on it."],[2212.25,"So this seems like a number of random things thrown together."],[2215.31,"In detail terms."],[2216.99,"Okay. First thing, he says, make an altar of earth."],[2226.21,"And then he includes if you use stones."],[2228.69,"So he's means you're making it out of natural things."],[2235.06,"Out of the ground, out of the earth."],[2237.8,"And then he reiterates you're not to use tools on the stones."],[2242.44,"How do you do it if you don't use tools?"],[2244.84,"You stack up rocks."],[2248.04,"You find rocks, stack them up."],[2250.44,"Yeah. Um. So what's the idea here?"],[2258.14,"The idea is this altar that you're making and the things that are sacrificed on it?"],[2265.62,"Are you worshipping God and giving back to God using the things that God has given you?"],[2276.23,"You're not offering your own efforts right to God."],[2283.19,"The tools are humanly made, and that's what the problem is."],[2287.32,"God is not going to be impressed with the really cool, beautiful altar you made."],[2294.41,"Now, why would you have to say that?"],[2296.49,"Because the pagan gods were very much impressed."],[2299.37,"They would go and build stuff to them and for them to impress them and get favors from them."],[2305.97,"Is there kind of an element of like because I know like uh I think it's the things uh giving an epithist where it says like you God is the host, like what like what a wonderful host you are."],[2317.57,"So is an element of this of like even though they're having to assemble it, it's still God's like stuff."],[2328.21,"Yeah, it's like his data."],[2329.65,"Yeah, yeah. And that's we say that in the liturgy."],[2332.31,"Thine own of thine own we offer unto thee."],[2334.41,"Anything we offer to God, we're really offering back to him because it's it's his and he gave it to us."],[2341.53,"And we're just offering it back."],[2344.33,"So it's right."],[2345.61,"It's not, we can't create anything to give him that he doesn't already have and doesn't already own."],[2352.59,"Is the idea here."],[2354.43,"Um, and that God isn't looking for you to perform these impressive, these. things that will impress him"],[2361.75,"This is Aristotle calls this the virtue of uh uh uh magnanimity."],[2368.47,"We go out and endow temples."],[2371.03,"Oh, I built a beautiful temple to this God."],[2373.03,"Now this God will honor me."],[2376.74,"And that's then related to notice he says you're to build these altars at every place where he places his name."],[2386.36,"That's where you build an altar."],[2387.96,"You build an altar where God goes."],[2392.44,"Which is the inverse again of that pagan idea."],[2395.56,"The pagan idea was I build the temple here, I build the idol here, I build the altar here, I make God come to me so that I can get what I want from him."],[2403.32,"I summon him here"],[2410.86,"Is there a moment of that in Solomon's temple?"],[2414.06,"Well when we get there, we'll talk about the problematic uh yeah."],[2417.9,"Solomon prays this prayer, and then God's response is not great prayer, Solomon."],[2423.5,"There's some corrective there in God's response in terms of how they look at it."],[2427.82,"Yeah. But uh that's a few books ahead of us."],[2430.86,"But yeah, but yeah, so this is God is going to come somewhere."],[2435.66,"He's gonna manifest himself in his presence there."],[2438.7,"You build an altar there."],[2439.9,"And that's the pattern we saw in Genesis."],[2441.66,"Remember, we said Genesis is the uh prelude to what goes on in the rest of the Torah."],[2447.34,"Remember that we saw. the patriarchs, Abraham, Isaac, Jacob, build altars at places where God appeared to them"],[2454.9,"God came and appeared to them, and they built an altar there, not vice versa."],[2459.88,"So God is saying that same thing."],[2462.9,"When I appear to you, you build an altar there and you build it out of the stuff I've given you."],[2469.58,"Um, this is not your effort in terms of worship."],[2476.14,"Um, so that this is reinforcing, and we're gonna see this reinforced in some stories when we get into Leviticus, that we worship God correctly by worshiping Him the way he's commanded us to worship H.im"],[2493.6,"Which runs counter to at least the broad American culture of what worship is."],[2502.8,"Where worship is, you know, I'm God's little kid, and I make him a finger painting, and I go and I hold it up to him and say, Look what I made."],[2512.7,"And that involves a fog machine somehow."],[2516.94,"But But it's not right our music, my musical expression, my poetic expression."],[2523.78,"I'm going to offer to God my skill."],[2528.51,"We worship God when we worship Him correctly, when we worship in the way he's commanded us to worship Him."],[2533.71,"Which makes sense"],[2536.23,"If worship is offering gifts."],[2538.89,"The example I've used before many a time."],[2542.41,"If I get my wife for our anniversary, a Makita cordless power drill."],[2549.21,"Okay. And I say to her, But honey, it was expensive."],[2555.46,"She's still not going to be impressed with that gift."],[2558.34,"Why? Well, because it's going to be obvious that I got her something I wanted."],[2564.28,"Something I would enjoy having."],[2568.62,"I didn't give her what she wanted."],[2571.82,"So it's not really a gift."],[2575.83,"So, in the same way, if worship is giving gifts, literal or metaphorical, to God."],[2583.29,"If you want to say praise."],[2584.91,"A sacrifice of praise."],[2587.36,"Then I'm going to be giving him the kind, the sort, the form that he wants, not the kind that I enjoy."],[2599.1,"If it's what I enjoy doing, then it's not about him, it's about me."],[2603.18,"Just like me buying the power drill and claiming it's an anniversary gift for my wife."],[2609.43,"And that would probably especially offend my wife."],[2612.15,"Why? Well, because I just used our anniversary as an excuse to buy myself something."],[2618.03,"Which is pretty darn selfish."],[2621.73,"That shows a kind of lack of caring and love for her."],[2626.29,"So when we go and do something we want to do and enjoy doing and say, this is me worshiping God, we're using the worship of God, say, on a Sunday morning as an excuse to do something we enjoy."],[2644.69,"We ought not to think that that doesn't manifest the same kind of lack of care and concern of love for God, despite what the lyrics of our singing may be."],[2655.51,"That that gift gift in scare quotes would be to my wife."],[2662.75,"So then also you're not supposed to have stairs right there at the end, because if you're wearing a robe and you walk upstairs, your lower parts will be exposed."],[2677.29,"So what's the point there?"],[2678.49,"Well, first of all, the stairs also factor into the whole me building something magnificent and impressive."],[2686.51,"So if I'm actually building it from Earth without tools and everything, it's gonna be crude."],[2691.31,"Not gonna need stairs."],[2692.6,"Like, I'm not gonna need to build a bunch of stairs leading to it."],[2696.7,"And so the comment about the nakedness is more of a sort of ironic comment."],[2704.72,"It's oh, you build this magnificent altar, you build these magnificent stairs leading up to it, you make your procession of it wearing your robes, and everybody can look up and see your right."],[2725.6,"There is a lot of humor and irony and puns."],[2731.73,"And plays on words and stuff all through the Hebrew Bible, including the Torah."],[2737.89,"Wow. Okay. And that is not me making light of the Bible or da-da-da-da-da-da-da-da."],[2745.93,"That's in here."],[2748.19,"That's in here."],[2749.79,"That kind of sarcasm and stuff like that is in here."],[2753.88,"And it's a way of communicating."],[2756.04,"Because humans get very puffed up and proud and big headed and pretentious about things."],[2762.58,"And start thinking a lot of themselves, and God comes and bursts their bubble."],[2769.06,"Because we all need our bubble burst from time to time."],[2772.82,"I have a Dutch family."],[2773.94,"That's their whole job."],[2775.7,"Is just to make sure I never develop self-esteem."],[2780.76,"But, you know."],[2782.64,"But a lot of the pun is lost in the English language."],[2785.98,"So you have to kind of picture it and imagine it."],[2789.36,"In order to get to why that comment about exposing your nakedness is there."],[2796.32,"It would probably make more sense in Greek or Hebrew."],[2799.2,"I mean, in well, no, you still have to imagine it."],[2802.08,"You still have to imagine it."],[2804.33,"And so again, what if we took this really literally?"],[2806.89,"Well, okay, it's okay to have stairs as long as you wear pants."],[2813.37,"See, see what I mean?"],[2815.05,"Like you missed the point."],[2817.79,"By trying to take it with that kind of goofy literalism."],[2822.99,"Listen next time as Father Stephen DeYoung continues his study of the scriptures on the Whole Counsel of God."],[2830.35,"Father Stephen's email address is wholecounsel at ancientfaith.com."],[2832.99,"unsel. That's wholeco at ancientfaith.com"]],"o":[{"t":20.96,"lv":2,"h":"Where we are: Ten Commandments to detailed laws","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\">Picking up at <b>Exodus 20:18</b>; last time: <b>Ten Commandments</b> (\"only sort of nine\")</li><li class=\"d0\">Ten Commandments = <b>microcosm / summary</b> of the whole covenant with Israel</li><li class=\"d0\">Rest of Torah = mix of kinds of material</li><li class=\"d1\"><b>narrative</b> - Israel's journey through the desert continues</li><li class=\"d1\"><b>commandments</b> - breaking the Ten down in more detail</li><li class=\"d1\"><b>liturgical instructions</b> - rest of Exodus = tabernacle furniture, \"how many cubits wide and high\"</li><li class=\"d0\">Tonight: mostly the commandments stemming from the Ten</li><li class=\"d0\">First time through, ~<b>11.5-12 years ago</b>: expected everyone to leave at end of Exodus / Leviticus - they didn't</li><li class=\"d1\">waving the <b>fatty part above the kidney</b> of the calf - less exciting, but \"something being said with that kind of stuff too\"</li><li class=\"d1\">repetitive bits (list of <b>unclean birds</b>) = move through quickly</li></ul>"},{"t":237.28,"lv":3,"h":"Aside: how long is a cubit?","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\">Cubit = <b>middle finger to elbow</b></li><li class=\"d1\">not standardized - \"your cubit might be longer than mine\"</li><li class=\"d1\">later quasi-standardized: <b>royal</b>, then <b>imperial</b> cubit</li><li class=\"d1\">like the English <b>foot</b> = the king's foot</li><li class=\"d1\">based on a \"perfect man\", perfectly proportioned</li><li class=\"d0\">Joke about a building that got <b>water damaged</b> in a storm - \"must have messed up somewhere\"</li></ul>"},{"t":335.41,"lv":2,"h":"20:18-19 - People tremble and stand afar off","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\"><b>20:18</b>: thunderings, lightning, <b>trumpet</b>, smoking mountain → they <b>tremble, stand afar off</b></li><li class=\"d0\">Had been warned: go near the mountain, <b>you die</b> - didn't need the warning</li><li class=\"d0\">Flip side of \"presence of God is dangerous to the sinner\"</li><li class=\"d1\">people in the Bible meeting God's presence don't say \"let me run up and give God a hug\"</li><li class=\"d1\"><b>terrified</b>, rip their clothes - even when an <b>angel</b> shows up</li><li class=\"d0\">Reason: <b>not</b> that God is mean, goes around smiting</li><li class=\"d1\">his presence brings <b>awareness of own sinfulness</b> → humbles them fast</li><li class=\"d0\"><b>20:19</b>: \"You speak with us, and we will hear, but let not God speak with us lest we die\"</li><li class=\"d1\">not only don't want to see him - even his <b>speaking</b> directly might kill</li></ul>"},{"t":459.03,"lv":3,"h":"Moses as mediator vs Christ as mediator","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\">This is the <b>mediator of the covenant</b> idea from <b>Hebrews</b></li><li class=\"d1\">God talks to Moses, Moses talks to the people</li><li class=\"d0\">People say \"Christ is mediator of the new covenant\" but mean something <b>totally different</b></li><li class=\"d1\">Christ dying for sins ≠ Moses - \"Moses didn't die for anyone's sins\"</li><li class=\"d1\">not \"they worshiped Moses, now we worship Jesus\"</li><li class=\"d0\"><b>Moses</b> = <b>buffer</b> between humans and God</li><li class=\"d0\"><b>Christ</b> is <b>not</b> the buffer - \"almost the opposite\"</li><li class=\"d1\">contra <b>Reformation theories</b>: God would wipe people out, Christ steps between, God talks to Jesus, Jesus to us</li><li class=\"d1\">Christ <b>reconciles</b> people to God, brings and presents them so they can <b>safely come into God's presence themselves</b></li><li class=\"d0\">Fundamental difference between Moses' role (old) and Christ's (new)</li></ul>"},{"t":597.5,"lv":3,"h":"Q&amp;A: praying to Jesus vs the Father","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\">Q: taught \"my prayers pass through Jesus so humans don't get it\"</li><li class=\"d1\">A: he <b>is</b> God - so that teaching was wrong</li><li class=\"d0\">Jesus teaches in the Gospels: pray to God <b>in his name</b></li><li class=\"d1\">can pray to <b>God the Father</b> - Christ says so explicitly in <b>John</b>: \"the Father himself loves you\"</li><li class=\"d1\">don't have to start prayer with Jesus</li><li class=\"d0\"><b>Liturgy</b> prayers: some to Jesus, most to <b>God the Father</b>, then in the name of the Trinity</li><li class=\"d1\">tell by the ending: \"<b>Thou</b> and thine only-begotten Son and thine all-holy Spirit\" - the thou = the Father</li><li class=\"d0\">Q: praying from prayer book vs own words - ends up \"Lord Jesus\" - ingrained; fine</li></ul>"},{"t":694.2,"lv":2,"h":"Moses an accommodation; Christ solves the problem","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\">Moses = mediator as <b>accommodation to a problem</b>: holy God, sinful people</li><li class=\"d1\">\"he is a buffer\" - Moses</li><li class=\"d0\">Christ <b>solves the underlying problem</b> - sinful people made <b>no longer sinful</b> → can enter God's presence</li><li class=\"d0\">Still call him mediator of the covenant - but meaning differs</li><li class=\"d1\"><b>not</b> running interference, serving as buffer, absorbing God's wrath</li><li class=\"d1\"><b>reconciling us to God</b> so we can come into his presence</li></ul>"},{"t":758.96,"lv":2,"h":"20:20-21 - Fear of the Lord","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\"><b>20:20</b>: \"Be of good courage, for God has come to <b>test</b> you, that his <b>fear</b> may be among you, so you may not sin\"</li><li class=\"d0\"><b>20:21</b>: people stand afar off; <b>Moses draws near the thick darkness</b> where God was</li><li class=\"d0\"><b>Fear of God</b> = awe, reverence, awareness of own sinfulness, humility</li><li class=\"d1\">when tempted to sin: \"remember how you're feeling right now. Remember who God is\"</li><li class=\"d0\"><b>St. John Chrysostom</b>: we sin without <b>fear and shame</b>, then fear and shame at <b>confession</b></li><li class=\"d1\">should be the <b>opposite</b>: afraid and ashamed to sin, <b>happy and bold</b> at confession where we're forgiven</li><li class=\"d1\">\"we have that backwards\" - same as Moses here</li><li class=\"d0\">Fear of the Lord does <b>not</b> mean being afraid of God, expecting to be smitten</li><li class=\"d1\">not Moses saying \"heathens, stay away\"</li></ul>"},{"t":888.94,"lv":3,"h":"Pagan familiarity and our overfamiliarity","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\"><b>Pagans</b> comfortable with idols: ritual, feed and dress the idol → get goodies back</li><li class=\"d1\">no reason to be scared walking into an idol's temple</li><li class=\"d1\"><b>Augustus</b> lost a legion in the <b>forest of Germany</b> → Romans threw rocks at temples of the gods: \"you didn't deliver when it counted\"</li><li class=\"d1\">still in <b>Latin America</b>: saint's image that didn't answer prayers put in a prison</li><li class=\"d0\">Our danger: <b>overfamiliar, presumptuous</b> attitude toward God, church, holy things</li><li class=\"d1\">treat casually, incl. sin: \"God'll forgive me, I'm only human, everybody does this\"</li><li class=\"d0\">That's the opposite of fear of the Lord</li></ul>"},{"t":1015.88,"lv":2,"h":"Fear of the Lord is the beginning of wisdom","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\"><b>Proverbs</b>: fear of the Lord = <b>beginning of wisdom</b> (\"we'll get to Proverbs eventually\")</li><li class=\"d1\">without it you're a <b>fool</b>, can't claim to know anything</li><li class=\"d1\">prerequisite; <b>then</b> wisdom for how to live and behave</li><li class=\"d0\">Opposite of \"the <b>fool</b> says in his heart there is no God\"</li><li class=\"d1\">no atheists when Proverbs was written</li><li class=\"d1\">\"no God\" = no reverence, you <b>act as if</b> there's no God</li><li class=\"d0\"><b>Modern atheists</b> the reverse: claim no God but act moral, give to charity</li><li class=\"d0\">Ancient fool: says \"of course there's a God\", offers sacrifices, then lives as if <b>no justice, no judgment</b> - \"drink and be merry\"</li></ul>"},{"t":1121.99,"lv":2,"h":"Q&amp;A: repentance is not feeling bad","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\">Q: not supposed to say \"I'm only human\" but also not beat yourself up?</li><li class=\"d1\">A: you're supposed to <b>repent</b>; feeling bad and repenting = <b>two different things</b></li><li class=\"d0\">Feeling bad might motivate some to repent - many just <b>wallow</b></li><li class=\"d1\"><b>despondency and despair = a vice, a sin</b> itself - not repentance</li><li class=\"d0\"><b>Repentance</b> = actually doing things</li><li class=\"d1\"><b>restitution</b> - will see in coming commandments: make it right as best you can</li><li class=\"d1\">fix what you broke, heal people you hurt - \"not just say sorry\"; repair the relationship</li><li class=\"d1\">takes time: hurt in a second, healing takes far longer</li><li class=\"d1\"><b>stop doing it</b> in future, to best of your ability</li><li class=\"d0\">Can do both and <b>never feel bad</b> - even be <b>joyful</b>: joy from knowing you'll be forgiven</li></ul>"},{"t":1255.32,"lv":3,"h":"Wanting others to feel bad; arguing about fault","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\">Biggest distraction from repentance and healing: <b>wanting people to feel bad</b></li><li class=\"d1\">someone sinned against us → we want them miserable \"because they made us miserable\"</li><li class=\"d1\">\"a kind of <b>revenge</b>\"</li><li class=\"d0\">Same in communities: hours arguing <b>whose fault</b> a problem is, rather than fixing it</li><li class=\"d1\">better: someone raises hand, \"okay, it's my fault. Now can we fix it?\"</li><li class=\"d0\">All comes from \"a really bad <b>Western view of sin and guilt</b>\"</li></ul>"},{"t":1331.43,"lv":3,"h":"Guilt is not helpful","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\">\"<b>Guilt is not helpful</b>. In any sense of the word\" - feeling it, assigning it</li><li class=\"d1\">runs counter to repentance (= healing, fixing, repairing, reconciling, moving on)</li><li class=\"d1\">can be a way to be <b>more self-absorbed</b> → despondency, despair, pride</li><li class=\"d0\">God can use guilt to bring you to repentance in some cases - like all bad things</li><li class=\"d0\"><b>Devil</b> uses guilt as a lever: next day \"remember when you did that? You're no good\"</li><li class=\"d1\">even after confession and forgiveness → keeps you from <b>taking the healing</b></li><li class=\"d0\">In these commandments: <b>nothing about feelings of guilt</b>, nothing even about <b>apologizing</b></li><li class=\"d1\">just: here's what to do to fix it, make <b>restitution</b></li><li class=\"d1\">\"apology tours in the media\"</li></ul>"},{"t":1478.79,"lv":2,"h":"Q&amp;A: where did \"feelings must match actions\" come from?","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\">Q: Christ wants intent and action aligned - where does emotion idea come from?</li><li class=\"d1\">A: \"there's nothing about <b>feelings</b>\" in Christ's teaching</li><li class=\"d0\">Mostly from <b>existential philosophy</b>, early <b>20th century</b></li><li class=\"d0\"><b>Motives</b> earlier: <b>Thomas Hobbes</b> (17th c.) - two springs of action, self-centered or other-centered</li><li class=\"d1\">dim view of humanity: gave to poor for treasure in heaven = \"selfish\", not good</li><li class=\"d1\">also out of <b>Calvinist total depravity</b> - must explain why people's good acts are never really good</li><li class=\"d0\"><b>Heidegger</b> → <b>Sartre</b> read in terms of emotions: <b>authenticity</b></li><li class=\"d1\">emotions not matching actions = not authentic → doing right when you don't feel like it \"not seen as doing the right thing anymore\"</li><li class=\"d1\">backwards: not feeling it but doing it is <b>more virtuous</b></li></ul>"},{"t":1639.46,"lv":2,"h":"Ethics before Freud; the cult of authenticity","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\">Basis of ethics for most of history: there's a <b>right thing to do</b>; moral person does it even if scared, unwilling</li><li class=\"d0\"><b>Freud</b>: humans have <b>instincts and desires</b>; unsatisfied = problem</li><li class=\"d1\">before: <b>not</b> satisfying desires made me moral - \"desire to punch a guy who insulted me, I don't\"</li><li class=\"d1\">Freud: unfulfilled sexual desire, <b>sublimated anger</b> → maladjusted</li><li class=\"d0\">Flows into existentialism: goal = be your <b>authentic self</b> all the time - \"that is BS\"</li><li class=\"d1\">\"I'm a sinner, my authentic self is pretty ugly\"</li><li class=\"d1\">fix isn't society telling me it's beautiful - it's <b>to be less ugly</b>, change</li><li class=\"d1\">don't display worst bits in public; keep private and work on them</li><li class=\"d0\"><b>Carl Jung</b>: \"whole other kettle of Gnostic fish\"; big split from Freud</li></ul>"},{"t":1798.8,"lv":2,"h":"Marriage: doing right without the feelings","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\">Now (<b>2024</b>, \"will seem quaint later\"): \"I don't feel the same for my spouse as 5 years ago, but I'm staying because it's right\"</li><li class=\"d1\">most Americans would say <b>wrong</b> - should leave, seek own happiness</li><li class=\"d1\">US <b>divorce rate</b> reflects this</li><li class=\"d0\">Weird shift: people feel <b>guilty for doing the right thing</b></li><li class=\"d0\">Over-analyzing motives: \"what's my motivation?\" - a lie</li><li class=\"d0\">Do what God calls you to do, <b>follow Christ</b>, hard or easy, whether you want to or not</li><li class=\"d1\"><b>authenticity comes after doing it</b> - you're transformed</li><li class=\"d1\">becomes natural \"along a process\"</li></ul>"},{"t":1967.81,"lv":2,"h":"20:22-23 - No gods of silver and gold","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\"><b>20:22-23</b>: tell the house of Jacob: \"you have seen that I talked with you from heaven\"; <b>do not make gods of silver and gold</b></li><li class=\"d0\">Expands the <b>start of the Ten Commandments</b> - first of the more specific commandments</li><li class=\"d0\">Shows the level of <b>literalism</b> to apply</li><li class=\"d1\">American push: \"interpret the Bible literally\"</li><li class=\"d1\">literal → fine to make idols if of <b>wood, clay, bronze, copper</b>? \"obviously not\"</li></ul>"},{"t":2058.7,"lv":3,"h":"Letter and spirit; the death penalty","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\">This is what <b>St. Paul</b> means by <b>letter and spirit of the law</b></li><li class=\"d0\">Idol made of clay: maybe not the letter - but violated what the <b>Spirit of God</b> teaches</li><li class=\"d0\">Coming tonight (hopefully): <b>first death penalty</b> - \"anyone who does this will be put to death\"</li><li class=\"d1\">one such: <b>cursing your mother or father</b></li><li class=\"d1\">did they execute every child who ever cursed a parent? Would wipe out the population - \"not many Israelites after a few generations\"</li><li class=\"d0\">\"Look, the letter\" - but already see that's <b>not how to read these</b></li><li class=\"d1\">\"he just doesn't want them made of gold and silver\" - doesn't make sense</li></ul>"},{"t":2185.37,"lv":2,"h":"20:24-26 - The altar of earth","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\"><b>20:24-26</b>: altar of <b>earth</b>; burnt offerings, peace offerings, sheep and calves</li><li class=\"d1\">\"in every place where I record my name, I will come to you and <b>bless</b> you\"</li><li class=\"d1\">stone altar: <b>no hewn stone</b> - \"if you use your tool on it, you defile it\"</li><li class=\"d1\"><b>no steps</b> up, \"that your nakedness may not be exposed\"</li><li class=\"d0\">Seems like random details thrown together</li><li class=\"d0\">Altar of <b>natural things</b>: earth, stones; no tools → \"you <b>stack up rocks</b>\"</li></ul>"},{"t":2250.44,"lv":3,"h":"Giving back what God gave","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\">Point: worship God by giving back <b>what he's given</b>; not offering your own efforts</li><li class=\"d1\">tools are <b>humanly made</b> - that's the problem</li><li class=\"d1\">God not \"impressed with the really cool, beautiful altar\"</li><li class=\"d0\">Need to say it because <b>pagan gods</b> were impressed - build things to <b>get favors</b></li><li class=\"d0\">Q: God as host - still his stuff? A: yes - liturgy: \"<b>Thine own of thine own</b> we offer unto thee\"</li><li class=\"d1\">can't create anything he doesn't already own</li><li class=\"d0\">Aristotle's <b>magnanimity</b>: endow temples → \"this God will honor me\"</li></ul>"},{"t":2376.74,"lv":3,"h":"Altar where God appears","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\">Build altars where <b>God places his name</b> - he goes, you build</li><li class=\"d1\">inverse of pagan idea: build temple/idol/altar here, <b>summon</b> him to get what I want</li><li class=\"d0\">Q: Solomon's temple? A: later - Solomon prays, God's response \"not great prayer, Solomon\" - some <b>corrective</b></li><li class=\"d0\">Pattern from <b>Genesis</b> (prelude to Torah): <b>Abraham, Isaac, Jacob</b> built altars where God appeared - \"not vice versa\"</li><li class=\"d0\">\"Build an altar there out of the stuff I've given you\" - not your effort in worship</li></ul>"},{"t":2476.14,"lv":2,"h":"Worship the way he commands: the power drill","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\">Reinforced in stories in <b>Leviticus</b>: worship correctly = <b>as he's commanded</b></li><li class=\"d1\">runs counter to American idea: \"I'm God's little kid, I make a <b>finger painting</b>\" - \"with a fog machine\"</li><li class=\"d1\">my music, poetry, skill offered to God</li><li class=\"d0\">Example: anniversary gift to my wife = <b>Makita cordless drill</b></li><li class=\"d1\">expensive, but obviously what <b>I</b> wanted → not a gift; excuse to buy myself something - selfish</li><li class=\"d0\">Worship = giving gifts (or a <b>sacrifice of praise</b>) → give the form <b>he</b> wants, not what I enjoy</li><li class=\"d1\">doing what I enjoy and calling it worship = excuse on Sunday morning → lack of care and love for God, \"despite the lyrics\"</li></ul>"},{"t":2662.75,"lv":2,"h":"No steps: the nakedness irony","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\">No stairs: robe → lower parts exposed</li><li class=\"d0\">Stairs also fit the \"<b>magnificent, impressive</b>\" building - crude earth altar needs no steps</li><li class=\"d0\">Nakedness comment = <b>ironic</b>: magnificent altar, stairs, robed procession, \"everybody can look up\"</li><li class=\"d0\">Lots of <b>humor, irony, puns, wordplay</b> in the Hebrew Bible, incl. Torah</li><li class=\"d1\">not making light of Bible; sarcasm is <b>in there</b> - a way of communicating</li><li class=\"d1\">humans get <b>puffed up, proud</b>; God \"bursts their bubble\" - \"we all need that\"</li><li class=\"d1\">much of the pun lost in English - must picture it</li><li class=\"d0\">Literalism again: \"fine to have stairs as long as you wear pants\" → \"you missed the point\"</li></ul>"}]});
